{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01EgH98WbxqnwgEPeHKc6tPB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Section 1 — Streaming & Real-Time Rendering (Answers)\n2\t\n3\tInterview-depth answers for Claude-style AI chat frontends: problem framing, approach, tradeoffs, concrete techniques, and pitfalls.\n4\t\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## Core\n8\t\n9\t### How do you render incomplete Markdown mid-stream without layout thrash?\n10\t\n11\t**Problem framing:** Tokens arrive as plain text, but the UI must show formatted Markdown (headings, lists, code fences, links). Re-parsing the entire message and replacing the DOM on every token causes **layout thrash**: reflow, scroll jumps, flickering list markers, and collapsed-then-expanded code blocks.\n12\t\n13\t**Approach:** Treat streaming render as a **stable incremental pipeline**, not “re-render the whole message.”\n14\t\n15\t1. **Parse incrementally** — Maintain a lexer/parser state machine (or use a streaming-capable parser) that classifies the buffer into *blocks* (paragraph, heading, list item, fenced code, table). Only re-parse from the last *incomplete* block boundary backward, not from byte zero. Libraries like `marked` are batch-oriented; production UIs often use **remark/rehype** on a block cache, or a custom tokenizer tuned for partial input.\n16\t\n17\t2. **Stable DOM mapping** — Assign each logical block a stable `key` (block index + type). Update block *content* in place (text nodes, `innerHTML` for inline only where safe) instead of unmounting/remounting the whole message. New blocks append; completed blocks freeze.\n18\t\n19\t3. **Defer expensive work** — Syntax highlighting (Shiki, Prism) runs on **completed** code fences only. Incomplete fences render as a plain `<pre>` with a monospace class; swap to highlighted tree once the closing ``` arrives.\n20\t\n21\t4. **Contain layout** — `content-visibility: auto`, `min-height` on message shells, and **scroll anchoring** (`overflow-anchor: auto` on the scroll container) reduce viewport jumps when block heights change.\n22\t\n23\t```mermaid\n24\tflowchart LR\n25\t  tokens[Token buffer] --> lexer[Incremental lexer]\n26\t  lexer --> blocks[Block list with stable keys]\n27\t  blocks --> dom[Patch per block]\n28\t  dom --> scroll[Scroll anchor + frozen completed blocks]\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\t**Tradeoffs:** Full AST correctness on partial Markdown is impossible (e.g. `*` might start emphasis or a list). Heuristics + “provisional” styling are acceptable; **correct on completion** is the bar. **Pitfall:** Using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` on the full message each tick — XSS risk and guaranteed thrash. **Pitfall:** Measuring height after every token for virtualization without caching block metrics.\n32\t\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t### How do you throttle token appends so the DOM doesn't repaint 50×/sec?\n36\t\n37\t**Problem framing:** Model output can arrive at 30–100+ small chunks per second. Each `setState` / DOM write can trigger style, layout, paint, and composite — blowing the 16ms frame budget.\n38\t\n39\t**Approach:** Separate **network ingestion** from **render commits** using a micro-batching layer:\n40\t\n41\t| Layer | Responsibility |\n42\t|--------|----------------|\n43\t| Network | Append to a string buffer / ring buffer immediately (never drop tokens) |\n44\t| Scheduler | Flush buffer to React state on `requestAnimationFrame` or `queueMicrotask` batch |\n45\t| Fallback | `setTimeout(16–32ms)` cap if rAF pauses (background tab) |\n46\t\n47\t**Concrete techniques:**\n48\t\n49\t- **`requestAnimationFrame` coalescing** — One state update per frame; merge all tokens since last frame into one append.\n50\t- **React 18 `startTransition`** — Mark streaming text updates as transitions so input and sidebar stay responsive.\n51\t- **Refs for hot path** — Hold the live string in a ref; commit to state every N ms or every frame; display layer reads ref for sub-frame smoothness in canvas/DOM hybrid designs.\n52\t- **CSS `contain: layout style`** on message bubbles to limit invalidation scope.\n53\t\n54\t```text\n55\ttokens --> [buffer] --rAF--> setState once/frame --> Markdown pipeline\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\t**Tradeoffs:** Batching adds 0–16ms visual latency — acceptable for chat; unacceptable for collaborative cursors. **Pitfall:** Batching only React but re-running Markdown parse on the full 50k-char message each frame — CPU bound, not paint bound. **Pitfall:** Using lodash `debounce(300ms)` — feels laggy; use rAF-aligned micro-batches instead.\n59\t\n60\t---\n61\t\n62\t### How do you implement a stop-generation button that actually stops the stream?\n63\t\n64\t**Problem framing:** “Stop” must halt **three** things: token delivery, server-side inference (cost/GPU), and client-side rendering/processing. Closing the UI spinner alone is insufficient.\n65\t\n66\t**Approach — end-to-end cancellation chain:**\n67\t\n68\t```mermaid\n69\tsequenceDiagram\n70\t  participant UI\n71\t  participant Client\n72\t  participant API\n73\t  participant Model\n74\t  UI->>Client: AbortController.abort()\n75\t  Client->>API: Close SSE / cancel fetch\n76\t  API->>Model: Cancel inference job (if supported)\n77\t  Client->>Client: Freeze buffer, finalize partial MD\n78\t```\n79\t\n80\t1. **Client** — Keep an `AbortController` per active stream; pass `signal` to `fetch()` (SSE over fetch) or call `eventSource.close()` and abort the underlying fetch. On abort, stop appending to the buffer and **finalize** the partial message (mark `status: cancelled`, run final Markdown pass).\n81\t\n82\t2. **Transport** — SSE: closing the connection signals the server (with well-implemented handlers). WebSocket: send a `cancel` / `stop` frame with `request_id` so the server can correlate.\n83\t\n84\t3. **Server** — Propagate cancellation to the LLM provider (`stream: true` streams often support closing the HTTP connection to stop billing). Maintain a **job registry** (`request_id → cancel fn`); on disconnect or explicit cancel API, call provider abort and persist partial assistant message.\n85\t\n86\t4. **UI** — Disable Stop after ack; show partial content with optional “Generation stopped” footer. Idempotency: ignore late tokens after abort (sequence numbers or `stream_closed` flag).\n87\t\n88\t**Tradeoffs:** Some providers drain buffered tokens after close — accept partial billing. **Pitfall:** Only aborting client state while the server keeps generating — wasted cost and race when tokens arrive after “stopped.” **Pitfall:** Not persisting partial messages — user loses work on refresh.\n89\t\n90\t---\n91\t\n92\t### What is the difference between SSE and WebSockets — when would you pick each?\n93\t\n94\t**SSE (Server-Sent Events)** — Unidirectional **server → client** over HTTP. Built on `text/event-stream`, automatic browser reconnect, simple `fetch` + ReadableStream or `EventSource`. Fits **LLM token streaming** where the client sends one prompt (POST) and reads many events.\n95\t\n96\t**WebSockets** — Full-duplex, persistent frame channel. Fits **bidirectional** chat: typing indicators, collaborative editing, tool progress from client and server, multiplexed rooms, binary frames.\n97\t\n98\t| Criterion | SSE | WebSocket |\n99\t|-----------|-----|-----------|\n100\t| Direction | Server → client (client starts via HTTP) | Bidirectional |\n101\t| HTTP/2 / proxies | Works through most CDNs; standard HTTP | Some proxies timeout idle WS |\n102\t| Reconnect | `EventSource` + `Last-Event-ID` | Manual heartbeat + resume protocol |\n103\t| Multiplexing | One stream per connection typically | Many logical channels per socket |\n104\t| Tooling | curl, HTTP logs, standard auth headers | Custom protocol |\n105\t\n106\t**When to pick SSE:** Default for **AI chat completion streams** (Claude/OpenAI-style), especially with HTTP/2, standard cookies/JWT on the initial POST, and infrastructure that prefers HTTP.\n107\t\n108\t**When to pick WebSockets:** Multiplayer features, **voice**, frequent client→server chunks, unified connection for notifications + chat + presence, or avoiding HTTP connection limits at very high concurrency.\n109\t\n110\t**Hybrid (common in production):** POST to start generation (SSE response body) + WebSocket for session sync, or SSE for tokens and REST for control (cancel, retry).\n111\t\n112\t**Pitfall:** Using WebSockets “for performance” when 99% of traffic is server-push — adds heartbeat complexity without benefit.\n113\t\n114\t---\n115\t\n116\t### How do you handle a stream that drops midway — reconnect, retry, or resume?\n117\t\n118\t**Problem framing:** Mobile networks, load balancers, and idle timeouts kill long streams. The product must choose among **reconnect**, **retry from scratch**, and **true resume** without duplicating or corrupting the assistant message.\n119\t\n120\t**Decision framework:**\n121\t\n122\t```text\n123\tDrop detected\n124\t    |\n125\t    +-- No message id / no cursor --> RETRY (new request, discard or hide partial)\n126\t    |\n127\t    +-- Server supports resume cursor --> RESUME (same generation, offset)\n128\t    |\n129\t    +-- SSE with Last-Event-ID --> RECONNECT (event replay if server buffers)\n130\t    |\n131\t    +-- Partial persisted in DB --> SHOW partial + \"Continue\" / manual Retry\n132\t```\n133\t\n134\t1. **Reconnect (SSE-native)** — Client tracks `lastEventId`; on disconnect, `EventSource` reconnects with header `Last-Event-ID`. Server must **buffer recent events** (Redis stream, short TTL) or replay from stored partial output. Works when the *same* generation is still running server-side.\n135\t\n136\t2. **Retry** — New API call with same user message; server may return a **new** completion (non-deterministic). Use idempotency keys if billing must dedupe. UX: “Connection lost — Retry” preserves user message; assistant bubble shows partial + regen.\n137\t\n138\t3. **Resume (best UX, hardest)** — Protocol: `stream_id`, `byte_offset` or `token_index`. Server continues generation from checkpoint (requires provider support or server-side buffering of prompt + partial completion). Anthropic/OpenAI often don’t expose mid-stream resume — **application-level** resume = append “continue from: …” with partial text (hacky) or store chunks and only fetch missing suffix from your own buffer.\n139\t\n140\t**Concrete client behavior:** Exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, cap 30s), max attempts, then surface error with **partial content retained** in local state and IndexedDB. Distinguish **user abort** vs **network drop** in analytics.\n141\t\n142\t**Pitfalls:** Blind retry creating duplicate assistant messages. Reconnect without idempotency producing duplicate paragraphs. Assuming resume when the server started a new job — always correlate with `message_id` / `generation_id`.\n143\t\n144\t---\n145\t\n146\t## Deep\n147\t\n148\t### How do you buffer tokens before flushing to the DOM — what's the right interval?\n149\t\n150\t**Problem framing:** Pure per-token rendering wastes frames; aggressive debouncing feels sluggish. The buffer sits between **network arrival rate** (variable) and **display refresh rate** (60Hz, or 120Hz).\n151\t\n152\t**Right interval (rule of thumb):**\n153\t\n154\t| Strategy | Interval | When |\n155\t|----------|----------|------|\n156\t| rAF-aligned | ~16ms (1 frame) | Default; smooth scroll, 60Hz displays |\n157\t| 2-frame batch | ~32ms | Heavy Markdown/highlight per flush |\n158\t| Adaptive | 16–48ms based on p95 parse time | Large messages, mobile |\n159\t| Max wait cap | 50–80ms | Ensure progress visible on slow devices even if frames skip |\n160\t\n161\t**Implementation pattern:**\n162\t\n163\t```javascript\n164\t// Pseudocode: dual constraint flush\n165\tflushIf:\n166\t  buffer.length >= 80 chars OR\n167\t  timeSinceLastFlush >= 32ms OR\n168\t  receivedStreamEnd\n169\tschedule: requestAnimationFrame(flush)\n170\t```\n171\t\n172\t**Adaptive backpressure-aware batching:** If Markdown parse + layout exceeds 8ms consistently, increase target interval to 48ms and/or parse fewer blocks per flush (see backpressure question).\n173\t\n174\t**Tradeoffs:** Character thresholds (e.g. 40 chars) smooth CJK vs Latin differently — prefer **time + rAF** as primary, char count as secondary to avoid stutter on slow token trickle. **Pitfall:** Fixed 300ms debounce — TTFT feels fine but mid-stream updates look jerky. **Pitfall:** Flushing on wall-clock only without rAF — tears and misalignment with scroll.\n175\t\n176\t---\n177\t\n178\t### How do you handle a code block that opens with ``` but the closing fence hasn't arrived yet?\n179\t\n180\t**Problem framing:** A premature closing fence guess wrong splits the block; treating everything as prose breaks formatting mid-stream. Code blocks are the highest-cost render (highlighting, copy button, line numbers).\n181\t\n182\t**Approach — explicit fence state machine:**\n183\t\n184\t```text\n185\tOUTSIDE_CODE --\"```lang\"--> IN_CODE (provisional)\n186\tIN_CODE --\"```\\n\" on own line--> OUTSIDE_CODE (finalize)\n187\tIN_CODE --more tokens--> IN_CODE (extend plain pre)\n188\t```\n189\t\n190\t1. **Provisional fence** — On opening ``` + optional language id, render a **single** `<pre><code class=\"language-xyz streaming\">` with unhighlighted text. Do not run Shiki until closed.\n191\t\n192\t2. **No false closes** — Require closing fence at line start (after newline), optional whitespace, then ```. Ignore triple backticks inside strings if you do mini-parsing; for interview simplicity: line-based fence detection matches CommonMark behavior.\n193\t\n194\t3. **Height stability** — `white-space: pre-wrap`, monospace font, min-height from line count × line-height avoids collapse when highlight swaps in.\n195\t\n196\t4. **Edge cases** — Nested fences in markdown (rare): outer fence wins until closed. Model emits ````` → show as literal until grammar resolves. **Streaming indicator:** subtle cursor or pulsing border on open fence only.\n197\t\n198\t5. **On close** — One-shot highlight (Web Worker for Shiki), add copy button, swap class from `streaming` to `highlighted`.\n199\t\n200\t**Tradeoffs:** Line-based detection fails on code that contains ``` in output — acceptable; show warning “code block may be incomplete” if stream ends in `IN_CODE`. **Pitfall:** Running highlight on every token inside the block — O(n²) re-highlight. **Pitfall:** Injecting HTML before escape — always escape code body, never parse as HTML.\n201\t\n202\t---\n203\t\n204\t### How do you render a streaming table where rows arrive one token at a time?\n205\t\n206\t**Problem framing:** Markdown tables need header row, separator `|---|`, and body rows. Token-by-token arrival means partial rows, misaligned columns, and huge reflow if the whole table re-parses.\n207\t\n208\t**Approach:**\n209\t\n210\t1. **Table sub-state machine** — After detecting `|...|`, enter `TABLE_PENDING` until separator line validates headers. Before separator, render as **plain preformatted** text (no `<table>` yet) to avoid invalid table DOM.\n211\t\n212\t2. **Row commit model** — Append a `<tr>` only when a full row line arrives (ends with `\\n` and starts/ends with `|`). Cells: split on `|`, trim, escape HTML. Incomplete last line: show in a **staging row** (`opacity: 0.6`) or keep in a `<pre>` below the table.\n213\t\n214\t3. **Column stability** — On first complete row, compute `columnCount` and `colgroup` widths (fixed % or `minmax`). Do not reshuffle columns as later rows reveal wider content — use `table-layout: fixed` and `text-overflow: ellipsis` until stream completes, then optional remeasure.\n215\t\n216\t4. **Performance** — For wide tables, virtualize **rows** inside the message (only visible `<tr>` in DOM). Header sticky within the message bubble.\n217\t\n218\t```text\n219\t| A | B |        ->  preformatted strip (no table)\n220\t| --- | --- |    ->  <table><thead>...\n221\t| 1 | 2 |        ->  <tbody><tr>...\n222\t| 3 | 4 | (no \\n) ->  staging row or pre\n223\t```\n224\t\n225\t**Tradeoffs:** GitHub-flavored Markdown tables only; HTML tables from model need sanitization. **Pitfall:** Building `<table>` before separator — header row jumps when separator arrives. **Pitfall:** Re-splitting entire table body on each token — quadratic work.\n226\t\n227\t---\n228\t\n229\t### How do you show a \"typing indicator\" that transitions seamlessly into real content?\n230\t\n231\t**Problem framing:** Users expect immediate feedback after send (TTFT). A bouncing dots loader that **disappears** then content **pops in** feels disjoint; the transition should feel like one continuous assistant “turn.”\n232\t\n233\t**Approach — single assistant message slot:**\n234\t\n235\t```mermaid\n236\tstateDiagram-v2\n237\t  [*] --> Placeholder: user sends\n238\t  Placeholder --> Streaming: first token\n239\t  Streaming --> Done: stream end\n240\t  Placeholder --> Done: empty response error\n241\t```\n242\t\n243\t1. **One bubble, multiple phases** — Create assistant message `id` immediately with `status: pending`. Content area shows typing indicator (CSS animation, optional “Thinking…” for reasoning models). **Same container** receives tokens — indicator removed on first token *inside* that container, not by unmounting the whole bubble.\n244\t\n245\t2. **Crossfade, not swap** — `opacity` transition: indicator fades 150–200ms as first text fades in. Avoid layout shift: reserve `min-height: 2.5rem` or match indicator height to one line of body text.\n246\t\n247\t3. **Reasoning / tool phases** — Replace dots with “Reading files…” or tool pill strip; first content token collapses only the **active** sub-indicator, not the full message chrome (avatar, actions).\n248\t\n249\t4. **Accessibility** — `aria-live=\"polite\"` on the message region; `aria-busy=\"true\"` until first content; announce “Assistant is responding” once.\n250\t\n251\t**Tradeoffs:** Showing indicator after TTFT &lt; 200ms can flash annoyingly — delay indicator 100–150ms unless reasoning UI always needs it. **Pitfall:** Separate DOM nodes for loader vs message — causes pop and scroll jump. **Pitfall:** Removing message shell on first token — breaks scroll anchor and message actions.\n252\t\n253\t---\n254\t\n255\t### How do you handle backpressure if the client is rendering slower than tokens arrive?\n256\t\n257\t**Problem framing:** Fast Wi-Fi + slow phone, or heavy Shiki/highlight, can make the **consumer** (parse + layout + paint) slower than the **producer** (tokens/sec). Unbounded buffers grow memory and increase lag between “true” stream position and what the user sees.\n258\t\n259\t**Approach — multi-layer backpressure:**\n260\t\n261\t```text\n262\tNetwork --> [ring buffer cap] --> [parse queue] --> [rAF display queue] --> DOM\n263\t                |                      |\n264\t                drop/coalesce          shed: skip highlight\n265\t                or pause read          extend batch interval\n266\t```\n267\t\n268\t1. **Bound the inbound buffer** — e.g. 256KB or 30s of tokens. When full: **pause TCP** by not reading from `ReadableStream` (`reader.read()` not called) — natural HTTP flow control — or cancel upstream with policy.\n269\t\n270\t2. **Coalesce in the buffer** — Merge tokens into larger chunks before parse queue; store “skipped display frames” but keep full text in the canonical string for final message persistence (never drop data for the saved message, only for intermediate frames).\n271\t\n272\t3. **Shed expensive work** — Skip syntax highlight, defer KaTeX, render plain text until `idle` callback or stream end. Increase flush interval adaptively when `performance.now() - lastFlush > budget`.\n273\t\n274\t4. **Catch-up mode** — If lag &gt; N seconds behind live buffer, jump UI to **latest** tail (user sees live edge) while background catches up persisted log — or show “Rendering…” with skip-to-end. Chat products usually prefer showing the **tail** (most recent tokens) for long delays.\n275\t\n276\t5. **Measure** — `tokens_received - tokens_rendered`, frame time p95, parse ms; alert if sustained gap &gt; 500ms.\n277\t\n278\t**Tradeoffs:** Pausing `read()` applies TCP backpressure — good for memory; may hit server write timeouts — coordinate with keep-alive/ping. **Pitfall:** Dropping tokens to “keep up” without merging into canonical store — corrupted final message. **Pitfall:** Unbounded `useState` string copies on every chunk — GC pressure; use mutable buffer + periodic immutable snapshot for React.\n279\t\n280\t**Libraries/patterns:** `ReadableStream` default queuing; `TransformStream` for coalescing; Web Worker for Markdown/highlight so main thread backpressure is parse-bound; `scheduler.postTask` (where available) for lower-priority highlight.\n281\t\n282\t---\n283\t\n284\t*Next section: [02 — Conversation State Management](./02-conversation-state-management.md) (when available).*\n285\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_013kJnkWnR17NyD6CiX3Kh1i","session_id":"deec6d0c-1337-4565-9116-ecd1be5fc177","uuid":"f396d7e4-d0ae-4830-825c-8370c134701d","timestamp":"2026-06-20T18:29:34.109Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write answers/01-checkout-flow-form-ux.md for payments-system"}
